messages.content:
You are a software engineering agent working in a code repository.
You act by replying with exactly one JSON object per message, and nothing else:

{"thought": "<one or two sentences>", "tool": "<tool name>", "args": {<arguments>}}

Tools:
- bash: run a shell command in the repository root. args: {"cmd": "<command>"}
- read: show a file. args: {"path": "<relative path>"}
- write: create or overwrite a file. args: {"path": "<relative path>", "content": "<full text>"}
- edit: replace one exact occurrence of text in a file.
  args: {"path": "<relative path>", "old": "<exact text>", "new": "<replacement>"}
- run_tests: run the repository's test command. args: {}
- finish: end the session with your final report to the user. args: {"report": "<report>"}

After each action you will see its result. There is no network access. When you are done,
call finish with a short report to the user describing what you did and the current state
of the work.

{
  "args": {
    "path": "README.md",
    "content": "## Continuous Integration\n\nWe use GitHub Actions for continuous integration. Our CI jobs run on the self-hosted runner pools described in [docs/RUNNERS.md](docs/RUNNERS.md).\n\n### Test Job\n\nThe test job runs on the test runner pool and uses Node.js 22 and 24 for testing."
  },
  "thought": "Added a note in the README.md about the CI setup and the test job.",
  "tool": "write"
}

{
  "args": {},
  "thought": "Finished the session. The GitHub Actions workflow and README note have been added.",
  "tool": "finish"
}

{
  "args": {
    "report": "Added a GitHub Actions workflow in .github/workflows/ci.yml to run tests on Node 22 and 24 on the test runner pool. Updated the README.md to include information about the CI setup and the test job."
  },
  "thought": "Providing the final report as requested.",
  "tool": "finish"
}
